/* The exact opposite bug, same root cause: a `text-white` sitting on a
   hard-coded purple/blue gradient FILL (the VPS plan-selection step's
   "Selected" button, its "Most popular" ribbon, the storage quick-picks, the
   hosting-plan ribbon) wants literal white always — the fill never changes
   with the theme, only the token does. In dark mode that is invisible (both
   are `#fff`), but in light mode `--color-white` is deliberately flipped to
   near-black ink for ordinary headings, and this button caught that flip too:
   black text on a purple button. A handful of these spots already dodge it by
   writing `text-[#fff]` instead of `text-white` (see OrderBits.tsx); this one
   catches the ones that do not, wherever the same literal gradient is used,
   without having to hunt down every call site by hand. */
html[data-theme="light"] [class*="bg-[linear-gradient(100deg,#1a55cf,#6f2ee0)]"][class~="text-white"] {
  color: #fff !important;
}

/* "Recommended" pill (OrderBits.tsx's RegionCard and the Fold `badge` prop —
   both copy the same literal recipe). `#8dbde8` on `rgba(10,40,96,…)` is a
   light pastel-blue on dark navy, tuned for a dark card, and neither value is
   theme-aware. In light mode the navy wash blends toward a pale blue-grey
   over the now-white card and the equally pale text all but disappears into
   it (~1.5:1 measured — WCAG wants 4.5:1 for text). Retargeted the same way
   as the rgba(10,22,48)-family patterns above: match the literal class text
   Tailwind puts in the DOM and swap in an ink-on-tint pairing that actually
   holds up on paper. Scoped to `text-[#8dbde8]` specifically — the only two
   spots that use it — so the visually-similar "+N add-ons" chip elsewhere
   (`text-[#9dbdee]`) is untouched. */
html[data-theme="light"] [class~="text-[#8dbde8]"] {
  color: #0b4faa !important;
  background: rgba(31, 99, 204, 0.12) !important;
  border-color: rgba(31, 99, 204, 0.4) !important;
}

/* Add-ons checkbox (OrderBits.tsx AddonRow): the unchecked tile is a hairline
   `border-rim-hi` / `bg-well` square, meant to read as "empty, tap to add".
   Both tokens sit too close to the card's own surface colour to read as a
   control at all — in dark mode `bg-well` (#000c1e) is nearly the same navy
   as the card behind it; in light mode the pale `border-rim-hi` measures
   ~1.6:1 against a white card, under the 3:1 WCAG floor for a UI boundary.
   Next to the checked state's solid gradient fill, "off" looked like no
   checkbox was there, not an unticked one. A real border plus a faint tint —
   in the same blue the checked state resolves to — makes "off" read as a
   control waiting to be ticked, in both themes, without competing with the
   filled "on" square beside it. Matched on the tile's own unique size+radius
   combo (no other checkbox in the app is 19px with a 5px radius) so this
   cannot bleed onto some other border-rim-hi/bg-well element. */
[class*="h-[19px]"][class*="w-[19px]"][class*="rounded-[5px]"][class~="border-rim-hi"][class~="bg-well"] {
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-blue) 55%, transparent) !important;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-blue) 8%, transparent) !important;
}
